{{smaller block|Have ye ''tippled'' drink more fine
Than mine host's Canary wine?
{{right|''Keats'', Lines on the Mermaid Tavern.}}}}

2. To affect by tippling, or frequent drinking; bring under the influence of strong drink; make boozy or drunk.

{{smaller block|If the head be well ''tippled'', he [Satan] gets in, and makes the eyes wanton, the tongue blasphemous, the hands ready to stab.
{{right|''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, II. 48.}}}}

{{smaller block|Merry, merry, merry, we sail from the east,
Half ''tippled'' at a rain-bow feast.
{{right|''Dryden'', Tyrannic Love, iv. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|He stole it, indeed, out of his own Bottles, rather than be rob'd of his Liquor. Misers use to ''tipple'' themselves so.
{{right|''Brome'', Jovial Crew, v.}}}}

'''tipple<sup>3</sup>''' (tip′l), ''n.'' [< ''tipple''<sup>3</sup>, ''v.''] Liquor taken in tippling; stimulating drink: sometimes used figuratively.

{{smaller block|While the ''tipple'' was paid for, all went merrily on.
{{right|''Sir R. L'Estrange.''}}}}

{{smaller block|Men who never enter a church . . . procure their ''tipple'' from a circulating library.
{{right|''Pop. Sci. Mo.'', XXII. 760.}}}}

'''tippler<sup>1</sup>''' (tip′lėr), ''n.'' [< ''tipple''<sup>2</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. One who or that which tipples or turns over; a tumbler. [Prov. Eng.]

{{smaller block|When they talk of a tumbler pigeon, you hear them say, "What a ''tippler'' he is!"
{{right|''Halliwell.''}}}}

2. Same as ''tipper''<sup>1</sup>, 1.

'''tippler<sup>2</sup>''' (tip′lėr), ''n.'' [< ''tipple''<sup>3</sup> + ''-er''<sup>1</sup>.] 1. One who tipples; especially, a person who drinks strong liquor habitually without positive drunkenness; a moderate toper.

{{smaller block|Gamesters, ''tipplers'', tavern-hunters, and other such dissolute people.
{{right|''Harman'', tr. of Beza, p. 313. (''Latham.'')}}}}

