{{smaller block|ally checkered for the firmer grasp of the shooter's left hand.}}

'''tip-stretcher''' (tip′strech″ėr), ''n.'' A machine for stretching hat-bodies.

'''tipsy''' (tip′si), ''a.'' [< ''tip''<sup>2</sup>, ''v.'', or ''tip''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.'', + ''-sy'' as in ''clumsy'', ''flimsy'', etc. Cf. G. dial. (Swiss) ''tips'', intoxication, ''tipseln'', fuddle with drink; cf. also ''tipple''<sup>3</sup>.] 1. Overcome with drink so as to stagger slightly; partially intoxicated; fuddled; boozy.

{{smaller block|The riot of the ''tipsy'' Bacchanals,<br>Tearing the Thracian singer in their rage.
{{right|''Shak.'', M. N. D., v. 1. 48.}}}}

2. Manifesting or characterized by tipsiness; proceeding from or giving rise to inebriation.

{{smaller block|Midnight shout, and revelry,<br>''Tipsy'' dance, and jollity.
{{right|''Milton'', Comus, l. 104.}}}}

'''tipsy-cake''' (tip′si-kāk), ''n.'' A kind of cake composed of pastry stuck with almonds, saturated with wine, and served with custard sauce; also, any stale cake similarly treated and served. It is used as a dessert.

'''tipsy-key''' (tip′si-kē), ''n.'' A watch-key, invented by Bréguet, having a pair of ratchets which clutch the pipe of the key when turned in the right direction, but slip when it is wrongly turned, so as to prevent any wrenching of the watch-movement. The principle has been applied to the winder in stem-winding watches.

'''tip-tilted''' (tip′til″ted), ''a.'' Having the tip or point tilted or turned up. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Lightly was her slender nose<br>''Tip-tilted'' like the petal of a flower.
{{right|''Tennyson'', Gareth and Lynette.}}}}

