'''tiptoe''' (tip′tō), ''n.'' [< ME. ''tipto''; < ''tip''<sup>1</sup> + ''toe.''] 1. The tip of a toe: used in the plural, with reference to posture or movement on the ends (balls) of the toes of both feet, literally or figuratively.

{{smaller block|He moste winke, so loude he wolde cryen,<br>And stonden on his ''tiptoon'' therwithal.
{{right|''Chaucer'', Nun's Priest's Tale, l. 487.}}}}

{{smaller block|Upon his ''tiptoes'' nicely up he went.
{{right|''Spenser'', Mother Hub. Tale, l. 1009.}}}}

{{smaller block|O how on ''tip-toes'' proudly mounts my muse!<br>Stalking a loftier gait than satires use.
{{right|''Marston'', Scourge of Villanie, ix. 5.}}}}

2. The ends of the toes collectively; the forward extremity of the foot, or of the feet jointly: in the phrase ''on tiptoe'' (''a tiptoe''), indicating cautious or mincing movement, or a stretching up to the greatest possible height: also used figuratively.

{{smaller block|He that outlives this day, and comes safe home,<br>Will stand ''a tiptoe'' when this day is named,<br>And rouse him at the name of Crispian.
{{right|''Shak.'', Hen. V., iv. 3. 42.}}}}

{{smaller block|They stoop forward when they should walk upright; they shuffle along ''a tip Toe'', curtesy on one Side.
{{right|''C. Shadwell'', Humours of the Army, ii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|Our enemies, . . . from being in a state of absolute despair, and on the point of evacuating America, are now ''on tiptoe.''
{{right|''Washington'', quoted in Bancroft's Hist. Const., I. 281.}}}}

{{smaller block|She . . . stept across the room ''on tip-toe'', as is the customary gait of elderly women. ''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, ii.}}

'''tiptoe''' (tip′tō), ''v. i.''; pret. and pp. ''tiptoed'', ppr. ''tiptoeing.'' [< ''tiptoe'', ''n.''] To go or move on the tips of the toes, or with a mincing gait, as from caution or eagerness.

readings.c2s3:
{{smaller block|Mabell ''tiptoed'' it to her door.
{{right|''Richardson'', Clarissa Harlowe, IV. xliv.}}}}

'''tiptoe''' (tip′tō), ''adv.'' [Abbr. of ''a tiptoe, on tiptoe.''] On tiptoe, literally or figuratively.

{{smaller block|Night's candles are burnt out, and jocund day<br>Stands ''tiptoe'' on the misty mountain tops.
{{right|''Shak.'', R. and J., iii. 5. 10.}}}}

'''tiptop''' (tip′top′), ''n.'' and ''a.'' [< ''tip''<sup>1</sup> + ''top''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''n.'' The extreme top; the highest point in altitude, excellence, etc. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|Everything that accomplishes a fine lady is practised to the last perfection. Madam, she herself is at the very ''tip top'' of it.
{{right|''Vanbrugh'', Journey to London, iii. 1.}}}}

{{smaller block|I needn't tell you, Mr. Transome, that it 's the apex, which, I take it, means the ''tip-top''—and nobody can get higher than that, I think.
{{right|''George Eliot'', Felix Holt, xvii.}}}}

'''II.''' ''a.'' Of the highest order or kind; most excellent; first-rate. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|What appeared amiss was ascribed to ''tip-top'' quality breeding.
{{right|''Goldsmith'', Vicar, ix.}}}}

'''tiptop''' (tip′top′), ''adv.'' [< ''tiptop'', ''a.''] In a tiptop manner; in the highest degree; to the top notch. [Colloq.]

{{smaller block|"That suits us ''tip-top'', ma'am," said the coxswain.
{{right|''The Century'', XXXV. 621.}}}}

